# Greek Philosophy: From the Presocratics to Aristotle

> Greek philosophy began when thinkers swapped mythos for logos —
> asking what nature, knowledge, and the good life are, using reason
> and argument alone.

## 1. The Presocratics: Explaining Nature by Nature

The first philosophers, working in Ionia and southern Italy, sought a
first principle (**arche**) behind the world's variety:

| Thinker | Approx. dates | Arche | Signature idea |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Thales | 624–546 BCE | Water | All is water; predicted an eclipse |
| Anaximander | 610–546 BCE | Apeiron (the boundless) | Cosmos separates out from an unlimited source |
| Heraclitus | 535–475 BCE | Fire | Everything flows; unity of opposites |
| Parmenides | 515–450 BCE | Being | Change is an illusion of the senses |
| Pythagoras | 570–495 BCE | Number | Reality is mathematical |
| Democritus | 460–370 BCE | Atoms | Indivisible particles moving in the void |

[definition]
**Mythos** explains by story and divine genealogy; **logos** by
publicly stateable reasons anyone can examine and dispute. The
Presocratic switch to logos is the birth of Western philosophy.
[/definition]

Parmenides and Heraclitus set the agenda: is reality one and
changeless, or many and in flux? Democritus' atoms answered both at
once — changeless particles, changing arrangements.

## 2. The Sophists and Socrates

The **Sophists** — travelling paid teachers such as **Protagoras**
("Man is the measure of all things") and Gorgias — taught rhetoric
and relativised truth. Plato blamed them for making the weaker
argument appear the stronger.

**Socrates** (469–399 BCE) wrote nothing; we know him through his
students **Plato**, **Xenophon**, and the comic playwright
**Aristophanes**. His contributions:

- The **elenchus** — a cross-examining method that tests a claim by
  drawing out its consequences until it agrees or contradicts itself.
- "Know thyself" turned philosophy from nature to the care of the soul;
  his sole wisdom was knowing what he did not know.
- Tried in 399 BCE for impiety and corrupting the young, he refused to
  escape and drank the hemlock, making the examined life his final
  argument.

## 3. Plato: The Theory of Forms

**Plato** (427–347 BCE), Socrates' greatest student, founded the
**Academy** — Europe's first school of higher learning. His two-world
metaphysics:

| Realm | Contents | Feature |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Forms (intelligible) | Justice itself, Equality itself | Eternal, perfect, non-material |
| Appearance (sensible) | Just acts, equal sticks | Changing copies that participate in Forms |

[example]
The allegory of the cave (Republic, Book VII): prisoners mistake
shadows for reality; one escapes, adjusts painfully to the fire and
sunlight, and sees the Form of the Good. Education is turning the
whole soul toward the light, not pouring facts into an empty vessel.
[/example]

In the **Republic**, the ideal state mirrors the **tripartite soul** —
reason should rule, spirit should enforce, appetite should obey:
philosopher-kings above guardians above producers, and justice is each
part doing its own work.

## 4. Aristotle: Reality and the Four Causes

**Aristotle** (384–322 BCE) studied twenty years at the Academy,
tutored Alexander the Great, and founded the **Lyceum**, whose
peripatetic researchers mapped biology, politics, and logic. Where
Plato separates Form from things, Aristotle's forms live *in* things:
knowledge begins with the senses — an early **empiricism**.

He asks of everything: why does it exist? — answered by the **four
causes**:

| Cause | Question | Example: a bronze statue |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Material | What is it made of? | Bronze |
| Formal | What is its structure? | The sculptor's design |
| Efficient | What produced it? | The sculptor's labour |
| Final | What is it for? | To honour a god |

His ethics locates the good in **eudaimonia** (flourishing), achieved
by virtue as a **golden mean** between excess and deficiency; his
**syllogism** founded logic as a discipline, unrivalled for millennia.

## 5. Hellenistic Schools: Philosophy as Therapy

After Alexander's conquests, philosophy reorganised around the art of
living:

@diagram tree id="philosophy-greek-schools" layout="tree" aria="Greek philosophy from Presocratics through classical to Hellenistic schools"
  "Greek philosophy" -> Presocratic
  "Greek philosophy" -> Classical
  "Greek philosophy" -> Hellenistic
  Classical -> Socrates
  Socrates -> Plato
  Plato -> Aristotle
  Hellenistic -> Stoicism
  Hellenistic -> Epicureanism
  Hellenistic -> Skepticism
  Hellenistic -> Cynicism
@/diagram

| School | Founder | Chief idea | Goal |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Stoicism | Zeno of Citium | Live by reason; control only what is up to you | Apatheia — freedom from destructive passion |
| Epicureanism | Epicurus | Modest pleasure; gods do not intervene; atomism | Ataraxia — tranquillity |
| Skepticism | Pyrrho | Suspend judgement on all non-evident matters | Peace of mind (epoché) |
| Cynicism | Diogenes of Sinope | Virtue alone suffices; reject convention | Self-sufficiency and freedom |

The later Stoa — **Epictetus** the ex-slave and emperor **Marcus
Aurelius** — made it a practical therapy of judgement.

## 6. Why the Greeks Still Matter

1. They set the problems and the vocabulary — substance, cause,
   virtue, category, syllogism.
2. His works returned to Europe through Islamic philosophers such as
   Avicenna and Averroes, dominating science until the 1600s.
3. The Socratic template — follow the argument wherever it leads —
   remains the discipline's working definition.

[summary]
The Presocratics replaced myth with logos, seeking the arche from
water to atoms. Socrates invented cross-examination and died for the
examined life. Plato split reality into Forms and copies and designed
the philosopher-king's state; Aristotle kept forms within things and
explained them by four causes, the golden mean, and syllogistic logic.
The Hellenistic schools — Stoic, Epicurean, Skeptic, Cynic — made
philosophy a therapy for living.
[/summary]
